:: Carter Halloween Spirit 1 [nobr]

/* Note: Unsure when this will be added to game properly, but be good to have set up. */

/* Written by: Zexion */
/* Intro */
The school day is finally winding down, and as you finish changing in the gym locker room, you give a contented sigh as you make your way into the halls. The noise of various kids chatting hits your ears as you make your way to the school entrance, and you think of all the things you're going to do once out of school. As you're a few feet from freedom, the familiar sound of your name being called echoes out in the crowded halls. You turn to see Carter jogging toward you, still slightly out of breath but looking more energized than usual. You pause and wait for the boy to catch up, curious about whatever new scheme or event has him so excited this time. <br>
<br>
/* Player: Mouth smirk
Carter: Mouth smile, Blush1 */
“Hey, $Player.Name! Wait up!” Carter stops in front of you, leaning on his knees for a moment before looking up with a bright smile. “I wanted to ask... since you’ve been in town for a while now, do you know how we celebrate Halloween around here?” You shake your head, making him grin harder. <br>
<br>
“Oh, you’re gonna love it! It’s not just the regular door-to-door trick-or-treating—though we do that too—but because we’re a tourist spot, the whole town goes crazy!” Carter begins to gesture wildly as he explains the various festivities, from haunted tours to community events. “The weekend before Halloween, they start the big setup at the City Park. My dad and the committee always need extra hands. I was wondering... would you want to help me set things up this weekend?” You nod, indicating your willingness to join him. Carter beams, clearly thrilled to have your company. “Awesome! Meet me at the park Saturday morning. It’s gonna be great!”<br>
<br>

[+end]

:: Carter Halloween Spirit 2 [nobr]

/* Note: Unsure when this will be added to game properly, but be good to have set up. */

/* Written by: Zexion */
/* Intro */
The City Park is a whirlwind of activity when you arrive on Saturday morning, with the town's littlest hero there to greet you at the entrance. You and Carter walk through the grass, navigating around groups of workers and volunteers. Carter points out various landmarks, regaling his friend with stories of Halloweens past, though his tone turns slightly more cautious as they approach a specific section of the park. <br>
<br>
/* PC: mouth smile
Carter: Outfit everyday, mouth smile*/
“And this is where they set up the haunted maze.” Carter stops before a skeletal framework of what will soon be a labyrinth of frights. One of the workers, a burly man holding a roll of duct tape, looks up and breaks into a wide grin upon seeing the boy. <br>
<br>
“Well, if it isn’t our local hero!” the man bellows, greeting Carter warmly. “Helping out again this year, I see? And you brought a friend this time!” The man laughs, looking between the two. “I hope you have better luck this year, kid. You aren't planning on taking any shortcuts through the entrenchment again, are you?”
<br>
<br>
Carter’s smile falters, replaced by a deep blush. The man turns to you with a mischievous glint in his eye. “He ever tell you about the time he got so spooked by one of the actors that he tried to bolt out a side exit? His costume got snagged on a fixture, and as he tried to run, the whole thing—underwear and all—got caught and torn straight off him. He reached the exit with a whole lot more 'spirit' than he intended!” <br>
<br>
/* PC: mouth smirk
Carter: mouth sad, blush3*/
The man roars with laughter while Carter’s face turns a shade of red that rivals the autumn leaves. “D-Dad’s calling! We have to go!” Carter squeaks, grabbing your hand and dragging them away as quickly as his legs can carry him. Once they are safely out of earshot, you stop, unable to contain a smirk. You nudge the boy and tilt your head playfully, silently asking how often random people in town get a free view of their "cute hero." <br>
<br>
“Cute—? S-Stop it!” Carter squeaks again, covering his face. “Just... ignore everything he said! Let's just get back to helping people!” <br>
<br>

<<linkreplace"Continue">>
    The two of you spend the next hour assisting various volunteers, moving boxes of decorations, and helping to hang signs. As they move toward the park exit, they encounter an older teenager struggling with a stack of heavy crates. Carter and you move to help, taking the weight off the older boy as they move the boxes toward a nearby trailer. The teen exhales a sigh of relief once the task is finished. <br>
    <br>
    “Thanks, guys. You’re lifesavers,” the teen says, wiping sweat from his forehead. He looks at Carter and chuckles. “I hope you have more luck this year, Carter. Try not to end up flashing anyone or getting your underwear stuck on a fence again, okay?” <br>
    <br>
    /* PC: mouth smile
    Carter: mouth sad, blush1*/
    As the teenager walks away, Carter stands frozen in embarrassment. Seeing the perfect opportunity, you reach out and give the boy’s behind a quick, playful pinch. You lean in and give him a flirtatious look, wondering aloud—through a raised eyebrow—if you'll get to see him in "less clothes" this Halloween. “I... I... maybe...” Carter mumbles, his voice barely a whisper as he turns his head away. Surprised, you lean closer and ask what he said, the boy snaps. “I have to go! I forgot I... have homework! Yeah, homework!” <br>
    <br>
    Carter spins around and begins to sprint toward the park exit. However, his legendary bad luck strikes once more. As he tries to dodge a nearby fence post, his belt loop catches a protruding hook. The sudden jerk sends him sprawling forward onto the grass, and with a loud rip, his shorts are torn and pulled down to his ankles. <br>
    <br>
    Sighing, you can't help but think that the old Carter luck strikes again. As you make your way over to him, you notice something different this time around. Instead of a typical bare bottom or camo-clad butt, you instead see a slightly more festive sight. Instead of his usual camouflage, he is wearing a pair of cute, Halloween-themed briefs covered in tiny pumpkins and ghosts. It's a departure from his usual set of briefs, but you can't help but think it looks good on him. <br>
    <br>
    /* PC: mouth smirk, blush1
    Carter: Bottoms none, eyes closed, tears watering, blush3*/
    “DANG IT!” Carter cries out, scrambling to pick himself up. He fumbles with his clothing, desperately pulling his ruined shorts back up over his themed underwear. Trying and failing to cover before he continues his retreat, disappearing behind the trees with his face as red as a tomato. Shaking your head in disbelief, you vaguely wonder how many people will get to see his new undies before he finds his dad, as you go and attempt to help your luck-deficient friend. <br>
    <br>
    Following the trail of crushed grass and the occasional frantic "Dang it!" echoing through the trees, you eventually find Carter huddled behind a large oak, his face buried in his hands. He's still fumbling with the shredded remains of his shorts, which are now held together more by hope than fabric, barely concealing those festive pumpkin-and-ghost-themed briefs. <br>
    <br>
    /* Carter: Hands bound, eyes open, tears none */
    You call out softly, leaning against the tree trunk, asking if he's ok, 'cause that was quite the landing. Carter jumps, nearly losing his grip on his ruined waistband again. "Don't look! Please tell me no one else saw that!" he squeaks, his face turning a shade of red that rivals his old favorite T-shirt. "First it’s dogs dragging me through thorn bushes, and now it’s fence posts! My luck is literally the worst in town".<br>
    <br>
    You can't help but chuckle, remembering the last time his "legendary bad luck" left him standing out in the open with nothing but his shoes. Well, at least this time you’re actually wearing something underneath, you point out. Gesturing vaguely toward the holiday undergarment, you tell him the ghosts are a nice touch for the maze setup, very thematic, you say. <br>
    <br>
    Carter groans, though he peeked through his fingers to see your smirk. "I was trying to be festive! Dad said I should get into the spirit of things since we’re helping the committee..." He trails off, eyes widening as he realizes something. "Oh man, Dad! He’s right over at the equipment trailers. If he sees me like this, he’s never going to let me live it down. He already warned me about 'shortcuts' after last year's incident with the entrenchment".<br>
    <br>
    He did say to stay away from anything that ends in 'embarrassment,' you remind him, recalling his father’s advice from the last park cleanup. "I know, I know!" Carter sighs, finally standing up and trying to pull his shirt down far enough to hide the pumpkin parade on his hips. He looks at you with a mixture of sheepishness and gratitude. "I guess I’m just lucky you were here to catch me. Again. You've seen me in my underwear so much I should probably just give you a season pass".<br>
    <br>
    Despite the humiliation, he manages a small, shaky smile. "Anyway, help me sneak back to the cruiser? If we’re fast, I can change into my spare shorts before Dad notices his son is currently a walking Halloween decoration". Carter tries to shuffle closer to the edge of the treeline, his hands still desperately clutching the shredded denim of his shorts. Every time a breeze caught the fabric, it fluttered precariously, threatening to give the nearby volunteers a full view of the "pumpkin parade" currently adorning his hips. <br>
    <br>
    [[Continue|Carter Halloween Spirit 3]]
<</linkreplace>>

:: Carter Halloween Spirit 3 [nobr]

/* Note: Unsure when this will be added to game properly, but be good to have set up. */

/* Written by: Zexion */
He peeked around the trunk, eyeing the distance between the grove and the line of city vehicles. The burly worker from earlier was still busy near the maze entrance, laughing with another volunteer. If they spotted Carter now, his reputation as the "Helpful kid" would be replaced by "The second coming of Jackson" before lunch. "Okay, here's the plan," Carter said, regaining a tiny bit of his usual mission-focused energy, despite being half-naked in a public park. "I'm going to use the equipment trailers as cover. If I can just get to the passenger side of the cruiser, I can slip inside and change. You just have to... I don't know, walk beside me? Like a human shield? A really tall, distracting human shield?" <br>
<br>
He looked up at you, his eyes still a bit watery but filled with that familiar, desperate hope he always had when he’d gotten himself into a mess. "Please? If Dad catches me like this, he’ll tell the story at the next three Thanksgiving dinners. He’s still talking about the time the ducks 'assaulted' me at the pond." You offer him a mock salute. Lead the way, you say, just try not to snag those ghosts on any more 'flat' surfaces on the way there. "Not funny!" he hissed, though a small, sheepish grin flickered on his face as he began his undignified, crouched-over shuffle toward the trailers. <br>
<br>
Carter continues his awkward, hunched-over waddle, keeping the massive white-and-orange equipment trailer between himself and the main thoroughfare where his dad is currently directing traffic. Every few steps, he has to pause and hitch up the tattered remains of his shorts, his face scrunching in concentration as he tries to keep the pumpkins and ghosts from making a guest appearance to the general public. <br>
<br>
"I swear," he whispers, glancing nervously toward the sound of a power drill nearby. "If I make it to that cruiser without being spotted, I’m never taking another shortcut as long as I live. Not even through a hallway at school! I’ll take the long way around every single time." You lean against the corner of the trailer, peering around to check the coast. He said that after the pond incident, too, you remind him, unable to resist a playful jab. And the thorn bush incident. And the time he tried to jump the drainage ditch. <br>
<br>
"Those were... technical errors!" Carter huffs, though he’s too busy trying to keep his dignity intact to be truly indignant. "This was a freak accident of physics. That hook shouldn't have even been there! It’s like the park is evolving specifically to snag my clothes." He takes a deep breath, steeling himself for the final stretch across the open gravel of the parking lot. The police cruiser is only twenty feet away, its black-and-white paint gleaming under the morning sun. His dad is currently occupied talking to the burly man, their backs turned to the vehicles. <br>
<br>

<<linkreplace"Go for it!">>
    <<set _random to random(1, 2)>>
    <<if _random == 1>>
        As Carter begins his high-speed, crouched-over shuffle toward the parking lot, his shredded denim shorts fluttering behind him like a tattered cape, luck—real, actual luck—finally deserts him. Just as he reaches the halfway point between the safety of the equipment trailer and the back door of the police cruiser, a loud, authoritative voice booms across the gravel lot. "Carter? Is that you?" <br>
        <br>
        /* PC: mouth sad, Carter: blush3 */
        Carter freezes mid-stride. He is caught in a perfect spotlight of morning sun, his hands desperately clutching the remnants of his waistband. Behind him, the seat of his shorts is completely gone, giving his father—and the burly volunteer—a crystal-clear view of the festive pumpkin-and-ghost-themed briefs underneath. His dad stops dead in his tracks, blinking slowly as he processes the sight of his son’s backside glowing with orange jack-o'-lanterns. The volunteer next to him lets out a snort that quickly turns into a full-bellied roar of laughter. <br>
        <br>
        "Nice... uh... nice ghosts, kid!" the volunteer wheezes, slapping his knee. "Very spooky!"<br>
        <br>
        "Oh, for the love of..." Carter’s dad sighs, dragging a hand over his face. He marches over, his heavy boots crunching on the gravel. "Carter, I told you specifically—specifically—to stay away from anything that could end in embarrassment today. We've been here for forty-five minutes. How did you manage to lose your pants already?"<br>
        <br>
        "It was a hook! A tactical hook!" Carter squeaks, not daring to turn around. He tries to pull his shirt lower, but it only succeeds in making the ghosts on his briefs look like they’re playing peek-a-boo. "I was just... getting into the spirit of things?"<br>
        <br>
        "You're in the spirit, alright," his dad mutters, though the sternness in his voice is losing ground to a suppressed grin. He reaches into his pocket and tosses the car keys to you. "Do me a favor and get him into the backseat before any more people see. The city isn't ready for this much 'spirit' yet." <br>
        <br>
        As you lead a mortified Carter toward the cruiser, he keeps his head tucked low, his face a shade of red that makes tomatoes look pale by comparison. "I'm moving," Carter whispers urgently as he scrambles into the car. "Tell my parents I've joined a monastery. Somewhere with no fences. And no holidays. Especially not this one." <br>
        <br>
        You shut the door on his rambling, catching his dad’s eye. The officer just shakes his head and gives you a weary thumbs-up. The "Helpful Lad" has struck again, and the legend of Carter's bad luck has officially gained a very festive new chapter. <br>
        <br>
    <<else>>
        "Okay, this is it," Carter mutters, looking up at you with those wide, watering eyes. "The final boss. If my dad turns around, I'm dead. I'll be 'Pumpkin-Butt Carter' until I graduate. You ready?" Without waiting for an answer, he bolts—or rather, he performs a high-speed, crouched-over shuffle that looks like a penguin trying to run a marathon. The shredded denim of his shorts flaps behind him like a cape, barely clinging to his waist. As he reaches the passenger door, his luck almost holds—until his fingers fumbled with the handle, and the click of the latch sounded like a gunshot in the quiet lot. <br>
        <br>
        Carter freezes, one hand on the door, the other desperately holding his tattered shorts up, his festive briefs practically glowing in the daylight. "Carter? That you?" his dad's voice calls out, getting closer. "DANG IT!" Carter squeaks, practically throwing himself into the footwell of the cruiser to hide. "Don't let him look in the window! Distract him! Tell him... tell him there's a loose duck near the maze or something!" Knowing time is of the essence, you quickly scan the area.  You call out, moving away from the cruiser to catch his dad’s attention. You ask if that’s a group of kids over by the unfinished maze section? It looks like they might be trying to climb the scaffolding. <br>
        <br>
        That does the trick. Carter’s dad sighs, adjusting his belt. "Not on my watch. Thanks for the heads-up. Tell Carter to stop daydreaming and get back to those equipment crates when he’s done at the car!" He marches off toward the maze, his focus entirely on the potential safety hazard. <br>
        <br>
        Your job done, you hiss for him to go now toward the open door.
        Carter doesn't need to be told twice. He scrambles into the backseat of the cruiser with a desperate, clumsy leap, his pumpkin-printed backside disappearing into the safety of the dark interior just as the door clicks shut. For a few minutes, the car rocks slightly as he frantically kicks off his ruined shorts and navigates the cramped space to pull on his spare shorts. <br>
        <br>
        Finally, the door pops open again. Carter steps out, looking significantly more composed in a pair of clean, non-damaged shorts. He’s still a bit breathless, and his face is still a lingering shade of pink, but the "Halloween spirit" is safely tucked away beneath rugged fabric. "Mission accomplished," he pants, leaning against the car door and wiping sweat from his forehead. "Seriously, you're a lifesaver. If he had seen my shorts... I would have had to move to a different state. Or at least a different school." <br>
        <br>
        He looks down at his new shorts, then back at you with a shy, appreciative smile. "I guess I owe you one. Again. Maybe after we're done here, I can buy you a soda from the vending machine? As a thank you for keeping the growing 'Legend of Carter's Bad Luck' a secret from my dad." <br>
        <br>
        /* PC: mouth happy Carter: Outfit everyday, mouth smile, blush1 */
        You give him a playful nudge, and the two of you head back toward the maze. The scene ends as Carter regains his usual bounce, ready to face the rest of the day—hopefully with both feet on the ground and his shorts firmly attached. <br>
        <br>
    <</if>>
